WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.350 align:start position:0%
 
mr brown thank you for joining us on the  program this morning um he's shouting in  the wind aren't you because the  government is not going to take any  notice  well look i'm standing in the cold  actually and it's been a cold night it's  going to be a cold weekend  and people are afraid to turn up their  heating because of the heating bills and  we're really dealing with that real live  situations where people are having to  choose between the food they buy and the  heating costs they have to face and  we've got to do something about it you  cannot leave millions of children  perhaps five million children in this  country in poverty  going to school sometimes ill-clad  sometimes hungry and something has got  to be done and i think the chancellor  forgot last week about that group of  people in our society who simply cannot  afford the bills they're having to pay  at the moment  and he's got to take urgent action to do  something about it and i want to see the  faith groups the churches the  foundations the charities mayors local  authorities all coming together to say  to the government look we have got to do  something about the condition that  millions of children and particularly  families but also some pensioners are  facing as a result of this escalating  rise in heating bills from friday this  week  when you were running for re-election  though back in  2010 your chancellor alistair darling  said that you'd make cuts i'm quoting  that were deeper and tougher than  margaret thatcher's in the 1980s  isn't that what the government is having  to do now they're going to have to make  those cuts because of the amount of  money that the pandemic has cost us as a  nation  no no and we had a winter fuel payment  for pensioners we had child tax credits  and we were continuing to give  families in need about 80 or 90 pounds a  week extra to meet their bills we  weren't cutting back on the benefits  available to people or the child benefit  child benefits gone down by 20 since  2010 the value of all benefits are  falling as a result of inflation rising  we're dealing with a completely  different situation here  when the government is cutting benefits  at a time  when the need is greatest and while we  introduce a winter fuel payment for  pensioners you're going to have to do  something about families generally in  this country who simply as i say we we  helped um with the local charity i'm  involved in we helped about 400 families  a few weeks ago and put 50 pounds on  their pre-payment gas and electricity  meter but we can only do so much uh  we've got a warehouse in in lock elliot  and fife where we're collecting things  from amazon and other firms that are  helping us uh give goods that can go to  families that are in poverty and that's  home furnishings but it's also nappies  it's also toiletries and it's also of  course uh going to be food as well and  we're working with all the food banks  but even all the goodwill in the world  and all the charity and all the work  that voluntary organizations are doing  can't bridge that gap which only the  government can do between the needs that  people have and the income that they're  receiving and you cannot raise benefits  by only three percent when inflation is  eight percent food inflation is ten  percent heating inflation is fifty  percent you've got to deal with the  situation here and now this is an  absolute crisis in living standards and  we're going to have the highest level of  poverty amongst children that we've seen  for 50 years  worse than in the 1980s when it was mass  unemployment that caused it now it's low  pay and low incomes that families are  receiving  but looking at the statistics um under  labour in  2010 the percentage of people in the uk  in absolute poverty after housing costs  was just over 20 percent it's 18  at the moment so you can extrapolate  from that that that late people were  worse off under a labour government  no you're completely wrong because child  poverty it went down one and a half  million under a labor government it's  now gone up to three million four  million it's going up to five million  under the conservatives look i keep  saying to you that child poverty is  worse than it was in the 1980s it's  going to go up from 4.3 million this  year to 4.8 million during the course of  this year and then it's going to be on  go beyond 5 million and the reason is  obvious we've cut the value of child  benefit by 20  in the last 10 years we've had benefit  cuts in seven of the last 10 years you  cannot keep taking this money away from  families  and then giving them huge heating bill  rises and then expecting them to pay for  the food shop which is going up in cost  we've just had the bread and milk are  going up as well  and expect people to be able to to fend  for themselves poverty is far worse now  than it was in 2010 i can tell you from  my own experience locally but i think  everybody who's listening to this  program knows that  there are you once you were of course  previously chancellor before you were  prime minister and you famously once  said there are two types of chancellor  those who fail and those who get out in  time do you think wishy sunax missed the  boat  i think you look i praised that sonic  when he did the furlough scheme and  i i wanted to be able to support  everything we're doing when we've got a  national crisis but you cannot support  the lack of compassion and the lack of  understanding about what's happening to  people in their real lives you know  we've got mothers in in fife where i am  who cannot afford a cucker  and therefore they can never give their  children hot food  i had a family that i uh  the cottage my family center was working  with where three children were sleeping  under one sheet they didn't have a bed  and they didn't even have blankets or  duvets now we are appealing today  for companies that can give us duvets  blankets and help with that sort of  heating  equipment that would allow people to  protect themselves against continuing  cold nights by the time we get to  september and we face another winter  with heating bills going up again the  crisis will be even deeper now no  compassionate chancellor can fail to  understand what is going on in the  despair that exists in the communities  that they just don't visit and the  desolation in the faces that quite  frankly they never see  do you have a thought on the police  fines  for events taking place in downing  street during the pandemic and the fact  that 20 fines have so far been issued we  are led to believe with more to come and  what that says about the culture in  downing street at the time  look i've got a thought about this when  people are suffering  when during the pandemic  people were not able to be at the  funerals of relatives and  my case and other cases  when people were making huge sacrifices  we had parties going on in downing  street and i think there was even a  party last night  what we've got to understand is the  government has a responsibility to all  the people of the country and if they  ask people to do things they've got to  be prepared to do things themselves and  take some moral lead and of course  that's what's been missing in the last  few months  talk to me about at the ochenton report  of course we've not got it yet but what  we do know is that several hundred  babies died that should not have died  because they weren't cared for properly  and then they weren't listened to after  their babies died or suffered from brain  damage i know that it's a subject that  is very close to your heart so i i am  aware of the sensitivity but i wondered  if you had a thought for those parents  and other you know would-be parents new  parents who are about to go into  hospital to have their babies  but no no family recovers from the loss  of a child  it leaves you um  grieving uh for the whole of your life  it's very difficult to come to terms  with that and if there have been  mistakes  and if people have uh  failed to listen to families after  the deaths have occurred then that's  something that really has to be remedied  quickly so there's going to have to be  new rules new standards but  i haven't seen this report i've got to  wait to see that report and so will  everybody else but what we know is  people are at the most vulnerable when  they have a  a child that they've longed for  who then dies within a few days it's  something that  no family should have to bear  and um of course as we know you and  sarah dealt with that with dignity and  and respect and i'm  i appreciate you taking the opportunity  to talk to us about that amongst other  things this morning i will let you get  in from the cold thank you for joining  us this morning mr brown  thank you

